<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RQFlinch - the right shoulder knockdown, compressed. One of six nodes covering the
  KNOCKDOWN LANE; the lane itself, and why it is reachable at all, is explained
  in hitreaction/RQFlinch.xml. Only this state's own facts are here.

  ENTRY. actiongroups/zombie/hitreaction/to_knockeddown-shotShoulderR.xml is the only
  doorway into this state - a repo-wide sweep of media/actiongroups found no
  second one - and it requires all of:
      bKnockedDown true, bOnFloor false, bStaggerBack false, bDead false,
      hitreaction == "ShotShoulderR", usePhysicHitReaction false
  A firearm critical hit from the LEFT or RIGHT supplies both halves at once:
  CombatManager.resolveHitReaction:2330 takes the headshot early-return only on
  Rand.Next(4) == 0, so three crits in four fall through to the ordinary table
  and can yield "ShotShoulderR" (:2350-2376), while
  IsoZombie.hitConsequences:3459 sets bKnockedDown from
  wielder.isCriticalHit() - the same flag, no second roll.

  WHAT IT COSTS UNCOVERED. Vanilla's node here carries no m_SpeedScale, so it
  runs at 1.0 and its only exit is
  <eventOccurred>ActiveAnimFinishing</eventOccurred> -> onground. A full
  animation, then the floor, then a getup. That is a Bulwark knocked down by a
  sidelong crit - the outcome this whole feature exists to prevent - and it
  survived the crit-compression slice because that slice covered the shothead
  chain, which a sidelong crit does not use.

  Copied from vanilla ShotShoulderStaggerR.xml, changed only by: our condition, an
  explicit priority (vanilla's node has none), speed 20, zeroed blends, and
  CancelKnockDown forced to true. Vanilla already cancels here, so that is a copy, not a change.
  FallOnFront is vanilla's value for this family and is load-bearing - it
  routes onground into the matching getup, and both getups carry RQFlinch nodes
  of their own.

  NO SetOnFloor EVENT, because vanilla's shoulder nodes have none - this
  family never marks the zombie on-floor from the animation. onground sets
  it on entry regardless (ZombieOnGroundState.java:41-43), so the state is
  reached correctly either way; the absence is vanilla's shape, not a gap.

  VANILLA SPLITS THIS STATE INTO TWO NODES on a FromBehind condition and we do
  not: the split only chooses between two clips of the same fall, and at 20x
  the choice is two frames long. Our single node wins both on priority.
-->
<animNode>
	<m_Name>RQFlinchKnockedDownShoulderR</m_Name>
	<m_AnimName>Zombie_ShotShoulderFall_R</m_AnimName>
	<m_DeferredBoneName>Bip01</m_DeferredBoneName>
	<m_deferredBoneAxis>Z</m_deferredBoneAxis>
	<m_Looped>false</m_Looped>
	<m_BlendTime>0.0</m_BlendTime>
	<m_BlendOutTime>0.0</m_BlendOutTime>
	<m_SpeedScale>20.00</m_SpeedScale>
	<m_ConditionPriority>100</m_ConditionPriority>
	<m_Conditions x_name="rqf1a2b3-75011-4000-8000-rftddirge75011">
		<m_Name>RQNoFlinch</m_Name>
		<m_Type>BOOL</m_Type>
		<m_Value>true</m_Value>
	</m_Conditions>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-75012-4000-8000-rftddirge75012">
		<m_EventName>FallOnFront</m_EventName>
		<m_Time>Start</m_Time>
		<m_ParameterValue>false</m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-75013-4000-8000-rftddirge75013">
		<m_EventName>CancelKnockDown</m_EventName>
		<m_Time>Start</m_Time>
		<m_ParameterValue>true</m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-75014-4000-8000-rftddirge75014">
		<m_EventName>SetState</m_EventName>
		<m_Time>Start</m_Time>
		<m_ParameterValue></m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
</animNode>
